{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Do not turn to the idols.</b> After warning about the fear of his three progenitors, he comes to diminish the idols, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:27): ״אוֹמְרִים לָעֵץ אָבִי אַתָּה וְלָאֶבֶן אַתְּ יְלִדְתָּנוּ״ — to tell you that there are no partners in your creation except these three and no more. And adjacent to this is ״וְכִי תִזְבְּחוּ זֶבַח שְׁלָמִים״, because he said ״אַל תִּפְנוּ״, and an unspecified turning is in the heart, as it is written ״וְאִם יִפְנֶה לְבָבְךָ״ (Deuteronomy 30:17), ״אֲשֶׁר לְבָבוֹ פֹנֶה״ (ibid. 29:17), for in idolatry the Holy One, blessed be He, joins thought to deed, as it is stated (Ezekiel 14:5) ״לְמַעַן תְּפוֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם״. And the reason for this is that the essence of idolatry depends upon the heart of the one who believes, and we do not find in the entire Torah that thought disqualifies like deed except in idolatry and in sacrifice — for if he thought at the time of slaughter to eat it outside its time or outside its place, it is an offensive thing, it shall not be accepted. Therefore he juxtaposed the commandment of ״אַל תִּפְנוּ אֶל הָאֱלִילִים״, which speaks of the turning of the heart, to the commandment of ״וְכִי תִזְבְּחוּ זֶבַח שְׁלָמִים לַה׳ לִרְצֹנְכֶם תִּזְבָּחֻהוּ״, which Rashi explained: do not think upon it a disqualifying thought, for in the entire Torah things that are in the heart are nothing, except for these two." "And as for what Rashi explained: and our Rabbis derived (Zevaḥim 47a) from here concerning one who engages with consecrated things in a disqualified manner, that he must intend to slaughter — with this we can settle the juxtaposition of ״וּבְקֻצְרְכֶם קְצִיר אַרְצְכֶם״ etc., to tell you that there is a commandment that does not require intention, and it is charity, as Rashi explained in parashat Ki Teitzei (24:19) on the verse ״וְשָׁכַחְתָּ עֹמֶר בַּשָּׂדֶה״ etc. ״לְמַעַן יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״ — Rashi explained: even though you came upon it without intending, say henceforth: a shekel fell from his hand and a poor person found it and sustains himself with it — behold, he is blessed on its account. And for this reason it is stated (Proverbs 21:3): ״עֲשֹׂה צְדָקָה וּמִשְׁפָּט נִבְחָר לַה׳ מִזָּבַח״, for the sacrifice requires intention and charity even without intending." "And what he wrote ״וּבְקֻצְרְכֶם״ in the plural, and afterward said ״לֹא תְכַלֶּה פְּאַת שָׂדְךָ״ in the singular — to tell you that even at harvest time when many are reaping, do not imagine in yourself to say: perhaps the poor person can sustain himself from the fields of others, even if I do not leave them ׁלֶקֶט, שִׁכְחָה, and פֵאָה — Scripture comes to teach you ״לֹא תְכַלֶּה״ and ״לֹא תְלַקֵּט״: the obligation devolves upon each individual, and he cannot exempt himself through what belongs to others. And he further hinted in this singular language, that charity must be in secret, so that it is not known to any person except the giver and the recipient. And some say: since the reapers are many, therefore he said ״וּבְקֻצְרְכֶם״, but in any case the obligation falls only upon the owner of the field; therefore it says ״לֹא תְכַלֶּה״. Thus does the connection of these verses appear in my eyes." "And we may say further, regarding the juxtaposition of ״לִרְצוֹנְכֶם תִּזְבָּחֻהוּ״ to the verse ״אַל תִּפְנוּ אֶל הָאֱלִילִים״, that they should not conduct themselves in their slaughter to HaShem according to the practice of idol worshippers, who slaughter only at a time when they are receiving benefit and attribute it to them, as it is written (Habakkuk 1:16): ״עַל כֵּן יְזַבֵּחַ לְחֶרְמוֹ וִיקַטֵּר לְמִכְמַרְתּוֹ, כִּי בָהֵמָּה שָׁמֵן חֶלְקוֹ וּמַאֲכָלוֹ בְּרִיאָה״. And this is not a sacrifice of willingness, but rather out of the compulsion of receiving benefits. But you shall not do so to HaShem; rather, ״לִרְצוֹנְכֶם תִּזְבָּחֻהוּ״ — as if you have no cause that compels you to the slaughter, and you shall have no alien intention, but rather you shall intend only to bring satisfaction of spirit before Him, blessed be He. And every alien thought is called outside its time and outside its place, for this is not its place and its time, and it is called one who engages with consecrated things without intending the essence and primary purpose of the slaughter itself. And this matter is clear to those who understand and close to hearing."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:02.450730193Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}